lessons.body:
Request Logging and Latency Metrics

A single request-completion record is more valuable than several disconnected “started / controller
entered / service entered” lines. Measure elapsed time using a monotonic clock and emit method,
normalized route, status, requestId, and duration.
const started = performance.now();
try {
return await next.handle().toPromise();
} finally {
logger.log('HTTP request completed', {
requestId,
method,
route,
statusCode,
durationMs: Math.round(performance.now() - started),
});
}

Metrics worth starting with
Metric                                                    Type / purpose

http.server.requests                                      Counter - request volume by method/route/status class

http.server.duration                                      Histogram - latency distribution

http.server.active_requests                               Gauge/up-down counter - current concurrency

taskflow.errors                                           Counter - grouped application error codes

process / runtime                                         CPU, memory, event-loop/runtime signals from
platform/instrumentation

Latency dashboards should use percentiles such as p50/p95/p99 rather than averages alone.
Histograms are useful because distributions can be aggregated across replicas; averaging
precomputed percentiles is statistically wrong.
Production caution: Keep metric attributes low-cardinality: method, normalized route, status class,
environment. Do not attach requestId or userId to every metric point.

